01.a|ADI|Partition image obligations into C17 freestanding semantics, AAPCS, Cortex-M exception/reset behavior, linker layout and board memory map. Derive data load/run addresses and BSS extent; classify the fixture's unresolved __aeabi_uidiv.|-ffreestanding does not prohibit compiler-generated library/helper calls.|Every image assumption has the correct authority; the fixture leaves exactly __aeabi_uidiv unresolved and no forbidden library yet linked.|Change integer width or optimization and predict new helper requirements.|Inspect undefined symbols before assuming an operation is inline.|Compiler headers are permitted while linked runtimes remain excluded. Language mode and dependency closure answer different questions.
01.b|BCF|Build original reset/vector/link/C entry with nonzero initialized data and deliberately pre-poisoned BSS. Compile copy,division and atomic operations likely to request helpers; supply allowed original implementations and audit final maps/symbols.|Zero-initialized emulator RAM can conceal a missing BSS clear.|The poisoned control fails without clearing, repaired startup restores the marker and all linked symbols have permitted origins.|Move data to another RAM region and revise load/run copying without clearing retained storage.|Give initialized, zeroed and retained regions separate linker symbols.|The actual startup loops use word transfers; alignment and size assumptions must be enforced by the link contract or handled explicitly.
01.c|DFI|Introduce one forbidden hosted dependency in a separate build, one wrong data load address and one helper with a boundary defect. Require distinct link-audit, startup and independent arithmetic failures before repair.|A failed build is not evidence that a semantic mutation test reached its target.|Each negative control fails at its intended gate; repaired code passes that gate with unchanged criteria.|Use the other compiler and re-audit generated calls after optimization.|Separate link closure from runtime semantics.|Strict target evidence includes generated code and final link identities. Host sanitizer libraries are verification tools, never target dependencies.
02.a|ABCF|Specify sixteen slots with one permanent idle and fifteen application slots, disjoint aligned stacks and atomic admission. Design a public slot+generation handle adapter over raw internal IDs, with exhaustion retirement.|Raw slot IDs and the capacity8 arithmetic fixture do not establish generation-safe runtime handles.|The small fixture use sequence3,7,7,4,6 remains separate; runtime admission preserves idle and rejects stale handles after reuse.|Allow startup-only versus dynamic admission and identify the additional serialization obligations.|Reserve every resource before publishing READY.|The retained port is startup-only. A new dynamic task API requires a separate proof and cannot inherit the startup assumption silently.
02.b|BCFG|Implement the handle adapter and stack admission using caller storage. Test tiny/misaligned/overlapping stacks, application exhaustion, failed partial reservation, generation saturation and refill; inspect the initial xPSR,PC,LR,EXC_RETURN and saved registers.|A stack pointer within RAM is not sufficient to create a valid resumable context.|All refusals preserve the prior pool; no old handle names a new task; initial frames match the actual switch path.|Provide an odd word count and require a precise top-of-stack alignment rule.|Validate the complete stack interval and frame before publishing it.|The port's frame layout is an implementation contract. Caller storage lifetime covers every suspended reference until deferred reclamation.
02.c|AFH|Reconcile static pool bytes, reserved PSP stacks, MSP handler stack and observed high-water marks. Include nested calls, exception alignment and extended FP frames in a separate analytical bound.|A paint-pattern high-water observation cannot prove maximum stack use.|Measured and derived quantities remain distinct; unexecuted paths and unwritten reserved frame areas are not counted as proven unused.|Add a compiler-generated spill-heavy path and explain why old high-water evidence expires.|List simultaneously live frames along permitted paths.|Stack budgeting is a call/interrupt-path problem. Pattern scans can miss reserved-but-unwritten lazy FP space.
03.a|ADI|Compare a true cooperative ABI-call switch with the retained stage1 yield-through-PendSV path. Derive which state is saved by caller, software and exception hardware in each; explain9-word payload36 rounded40 only for the fixture model.|Cooperative scheduling does not imply the implementation switches solely at an ordinary function-call boundary.|Both mechanisms have complete but different state ledgers; no exception-mediated test is relabeled as evidence of a separate call-boundary implementation.|Add FP use and revise the cooperative preserved-state contract.|Identify the actual instruction that transfers control.|The retained context.S uses an exception frame even when tasks voluntarily yield. The requested direct cooperative assembly exercise remains distinct learner work.
03.b|BDF|Implement an original Thumb call-boundary switch in a separate target variant using the selected AAPCS, disjoint stacks and controlled entry-return trampoline. Exercise nested C calls and preserved-register sentinels; retain disassembly.|A model swapping stack-pointer integers does not execute a context switch.|Repeated actual target switches preserve required registers/locals and returning entry reaches controlled exit.|Change optimization and frame-pointer use while preserving the public ABI.|Start from a minimal two-context ping-pong with observable counters.|Caller-clobbered and callee-preserved state differ from asynchronous interruption. Assembly must state compiler and ABI integration assumptions.
03.c|DFJ|Remove one preserved-register restore in a disposable variant and use assembly sentinels that the compiler cannot accidentally avoid. Compare the direct switch with PendSV-mediated cooperative execution and diagnose the first wrong register.|Two increasing counters can conceal context corruption if no live value occupies the lost register.|The targeted mutant fails a named sentinel; repaired execution preserves nested locals and stack ownership.|Withhold a return-trampoline defect for delayed diagnosis.|Make the corrupted register independently observable before and after transfer.|An apparent pass from unused state is a weak test. Distinct contexts exist despite a single executing CPU.
04.a|ABF|Define task states and legal transitions, runnable membership and permanent idle selection. State the serialization domain around combined queue/current updates; model dispatch,block,wake,dispatch,exit,reap.|A correct enum value does not prevent duplicate queue membership or a task executing while marked blocked.|The fixture states running,blocked,ready,running,exited,free match; each task has one consistent owner/state.|Replace an explicit ready queue with a scan while preserving semantic invariants.|Check membership and current identity together.|Internal data structures may change while the abstract scheduler relation stays fixed. Serialization is a precondition, not supplied automatically by model functions.
04.b|BCF|Implement transitions with invariant checks after every operation; enumerate bounded sequences containing repeated wake, repeated block, exit while waiting and yield with only idle available. Preserve state on illegal calls.|Rejecting an operation after partially unlinking a task corrupts later scheduling.|Illegal sequences refuse without state damage; no blocked/exited task runs and ordinary runnable tasks exclude idle.|Add priority selection while retaining lifecycle checks.|Snapshot state around each rejected transition.|A bounded exhaustive sequence campaign supports its stated horizon only; it should nevertheless catch transitions omitted by nominal examples.
04.c|AFG|Reconstruct per-task service and waiting from a transition trace; inject wake/cancel/timeout in all serialized orders and assert one winning completion. Compare task.result delivery with an API that incorrectly re-enters a blocked call.|CMX_BLOCKED is pending completion, not a request to retry the enrollment operation.|Exactly one result is published; no duplicate wait node or lost runnable task appears.|Move completion delivery into a port wrapper that resumes the suspended call.|Distinguish enrollment from continuation.|Hosted calls are serialized and cannot establish interrupt exclusion. The target wrapper must maintain the same abstract completion relation.
05.a|ABCF|Derive uint32 modular deadline order only for distances strictly below2^31; test exact due, just-before, wrap, half-range ambiguity and forbidden large clock jumps. Define equal-deadline insertion identity and cancellation generation.|Signed-looking subtraction does not impose a valid global order on arbitrary modular timestamps.|Half-range/large-jump cases are rejected or outside explicit preconditions; exact deadline is due and ties are stable.|Use a wider counter and retain the same bounded-distance proof.|Draw the compared points on a modular circle.|Integer representation alone does not justify time ordering. The API must enforce or document its admissible horizon.
05.b|BCFG|Implement a bounded absolute-deadline queue with stable order and generation-checked cancellation. Cancel and reuse a slot, then deliver its old expiry; inject full capacity and sequence saturation.|Canceling by recycled slot alone can remove a new timer.|No stale event affects the new timer; capacity/sequence failure preserves earlier timers and order.|Combine timer expiry with successful resource acquisition at the same logical instant.|Use one serialized completion rule across both mechanisms.|The common runtime permits immediate acquisition despite an expired waiting deadline; this is a deliberate contract, not inconsistent time arithmetic.
05.c|AFH|Derive absolute releases10,20,30 versus delay-after-work10,23,39 for work3,6,2. Add an overrun exceeding one period and specify skip,catch-up or fail policy with bounded processing.|Phase preservation alone does not decide what happens after missed releases.|Nominal fixture matches; overrun policy is explicit and cannot create an unbounded catch-up loop.|Change work to a bursty sequence and analyze release jitter separately from execution time.|Keep intended release and actual start as distinct timestamps.|A periodic arithmetic oracle is not a hardware timer test. Resource and deadline consequences of catch-up belong in the contract.
06.a|ABF|Draw hardware basic frame, software r4-r11/LR frame, PSP/MSP use and EXC_RETURN for SVC startup and PendSV. Compare fixture payload68/aligned72 with the actual saved PSP and public C-call MSP alignment.|Rounding an abstract total does not locate padding in the real exception frame.|Every saved/restored offset and alignment premise matches disassembly; architecture padding is distinguished from software payload.|Add extended FP state and rederive frame interpretation from EXC_RETURN.|Do not use a total byte count as an offset proof.|AAPCS governs C-call alignment; Cortex exception rules govern hardware stacking. Their sources and obligations remain separate.
06.b|BDF|Implement original SVC/SysTick/PendSV target flow and run two CPU loops that never yield. Record exception priorities, PSP/MSP, switch causes and register sentinels; mutate EXC_RETURN or one saved register separately.|A cooperative workload or host model does not establish asynchronous preemption.|Both nonyielding tasks progress through timer-caused switches with intact state; each targeted faulty image fails its own observation.|Add an ISR nesting point while keeping PendSV at the declared lowest priority.|Use counters updated without invoking yield or blocking services.|The mechanism must preserve interrupted state at arbitrary instruction boundaries. A compiler/ABI change may invalidate unrecorded assembly assumptions.
06.c|AFHI|Correlate timer interrupts, pending switches and completed context switches; measure observed latency without claiming a physical bound. Explain why a pending PendSV may wait behind higher-priority handlers or masks.|Tick count, switch count and elapsed time are not interchangeable.|The trace accounts for deferred/coalesced requests and identifies masked intervals; QEMU results remain emulator evidence.|Introduce a bounded long ISR and distinguish ISR interference from scheduler policy.|Record event causes and nesting, not only final counters.|A finite run demonstrates behavior under its environment. WCET and hardware interrupt timing need later calibrated evidence.
07.a|ACFI|Derive nested saved-mask semantics and BASEPRI encoding. Replay initial0→80→48→80→0, then enter a weaker threshold under a stronger existing mask; compare with the retained PRIMASK save/restore implementation.|Writing a requested threshold can weaken an existing critical section; blindly enabling on exit breaks nesting.|Nested exits restore incoming state; stronger existing protection is never weakened; BASEPRI and PRIMASK contracts are not conflated.|Change implemented priority bits and reject unrepresentable threshold assumptions.|Treat zero as the special unmasked BASEPRI value.|The finite fixture models threshold ordering. Actual register encoding and instruction choice need selected-core evidence.
07.b|BCFG|Implement a nonblocking ISR publication and deferred task reschedule path; inject interrupt arrival before data write, before publication, before pending-set and around unlock. Reject blocking wrappers in handler mode or any forbidden mask state.|A published flag without a complete data/ordering contract can lose work or expose partial payload.|Every accepted event is processed under the declared coalescing/counting policy; no illegal context enrolls a waiter.|Make the ISR producer reentrant and identify why the former single-producer proof fails.|State whether multiple notifications represent one flag or multiple events.|The target wrappers check IPSR/PRIMASK/BASEPRI/FAULTMASK. Passing the serialized host model does not establish those machine checks.
07.c|ADH|Measure mask intervals using an explicit clock and instrumentation overhead, then derive an analytical bound from permitted calls. Diagnose an inner-unlock-enable mutant and a blocking-in-ISR attempt.|The longest observed interval is not the maximum possible interval.|The mutant violates saved-state restoration; illegal blocking refuses; measured maximum and analytical assumptions are separate.|Add one logging call and show how dependency/latency evidence must change.|Include every nested critical path and error path.|Critical sections establish exclusion only against the selected interrupt classes. More urgent handlers need independent permitted-API rules.
08.a|ADI|Map low/high FP registers, FPSCR, EXC_RETURN and lazy-stacking control to ownership by hardware/software. Distinguish subroutine ABI preservation from arbitrary preemption preservation; account for reserved versus written space.|Saving only ABI callee-preserved FP state cannot by itself preserve an interrupted FP task.|Every live FP state component has an owner; the204-byte/208-aligned fixture is scoped to its selected combined frame model.|Change eager/lazy configuration and predict timing and stack-observation differences.|First decide whether the switch is a call or an exception.|The inspected AAPCS passage supplies call conventions, not the full Cortex exception protocol. Exact architecture configuration remains a required authority.
08.b|BCF|Run two FP tasks with distinct sentinels across all relevant high registers plus an integer task; retain compiler FPU/float-ABI flags and handler disassembly. Deliberately omit one high-register restore.|The retained s16 helper alone cannot demonstrate preservation of every high FP register or hidden FP handler use.|Each required register survives repeated preemption; the omitted-register mutant fails at that register and integer-only control is insufficient.|Use a calculation that changes FPSCR state and define per-task versus shared policy.|Use assembly-supported observations for registers the compiler might otherwise leave unused.|The target experiment extends the finite retained anchor. Accidental compiler FP instructions can alter lazy-frame behavior and must be inspected.
08.c|AFH|Compare eager/lazy traces and stack paint with explicit reserved-frame accounting; inspect the first FP instruction in an exception and quantify observed added work separately from analytical storage bounds.|Unchanged paint does not imply no reserved stack, and one low latency sample does not bound lazy stacking.|The resource ledger includes basic/extended/software frames and alignment under each declared path; no physical WCET is inferred.|Introduce nested exceptions and recompute simultaneously live MSP/PSP storage.|Track reservation and materialization as separate events.|Lazy state preservation changes when cost is paid. A correct result can still violate the stack budget under another exception path.
09.a|ABF|Define exit,result publication,join ownership,dispatch-away and reap states. Explain why the reference-count fixture2,1,0 is only a lifetime analogy and cannot prove the exiting stack is inactive.|A zero count or terminal flag alone does not establish machine quiescence.|Reclamation requires both logical no-users and actual switch-away; one joiner consumes one result.|Allow detached tasks and specify who performs final reap.|Name the context performing each cleanup action.|The runtime must not free or overwrite the stack containing its current cleanup code. Logical and execution ownership compose.
09.b|BCFG|Implement exit trampoline and single-joiner rules with exit-before-join, join-before-exit,self join,two joiners and stale handles. Defer stack release until another context owns execution; inject cancellation of a waiting joiner.|Exiting while owning an inconsistent mutex cannot be repaired by unconditional release.|Exactly one permitted result delivery occurs; failed joins preserve state; owned-resource exit refusal remains explicit.|Add a supervisor policy for faulted resource owners and keep poison distinct from normal release.|Record result ownership separately from stack ownership.|The retained model refuses certain owned resources on exit. A new robust policy needs explicit invariant repair before reuse.
09.c|DFJ|Exhaust/reuse task slots while preserving old handles and result buffers. Reconcile every stack through completion and verify no old handle observes the new task result; test generation saturation.|Successful refill does not establish stale-reference safety.|Each stack has a complete lifetime ledger and retired generations do not wrap into validity.|Move task results into caller-owned storage and derive its required lifetime.|Compare identities before and after reuse, not only slot numbers.|The delayed assessment should combine identity and reclamation under changed storage placement, not merely change pool capacity.
10.a|ACG|Specify cancellation request, safe-point observation,wait removal,cleanup,exit and fault states. Replay cancel/wake/timeout in all serialized orders and preserve first terminal completion and first fault record.|Cancellation is not arbitrary forced cleanup, and a task fault is not necessarily a safely recoverable shared-state event.|One winning completion and one cleanup path exist; owned inconsistent resources become explicitly poisoned or remain refused.|Cancel during a multi-step shared mutation and locate safe rollback/commit boundaries.|Separate request acceptance from task quiescence.|The nominal fixture picks cancel first. Its one order does not cover the other causal histories or machine fault containment.
10.b|BDFG|Implement a retained first-fault record and task-local faulted-state adapter in a dedicated teaching variant. Compare with the existing cmx_fault/stack-canary path that stops the whole image; inject bounded stack-bound and blocked-cancel cases.|A global diagnostic halt does not satisfy the module's stronger peer-continuation fault-containment objective.|The new variant demonstrates one terminated task, preserved first cause and continuing safe peers, or explicitly reports a fatal unsafe-to-resume condition.|Fault a task owning a protected resource and require a supervisor recovery policy before peers use it.|Do not relabel the baseline global stop as task-local containment.|This is required new implementation beyond the retained target anchor. No production board or arbitrary host memory corruption is involved.
10.c|AFJ|Assess containment with a canary mutation, an out-of-range saved-stack model and a dedicated target fault, keeping their evidence distinct. Account for wait queues,owned resources,result consumers and reaping after each path.|A canary detects selected damage after the fact; it is not an MPU guard or universal memory safety.|No canceled/faulted task remains runnable or queued; first evidence is retained and every unresolved poisoned resource is listed.|Replace canary-only checking with a selected MPU guard and identify architecture/setup claims needing new evidence.|Verify surviving peers' invariants, not only that they increment counters.|Continuation is safe only when shared state has a justified recovery condition. Some faults appropriately require whole-runtime stop.
11.a|ABCF|Derive timer counts using ceiling division:101us at32768Hz requires4counts, actual duration4000000/32768us. Check multiplication overflow, maximum reload, zero/no deadline and half-range time restrictions.|Truncating conversion can expire early; programming a clamped timer does not mean the original deadline was reached.|No early logical expiry occurs; long waits are split and residual time retained under a declared timer model.|Use a timer whose reload encoding represents counts-minus-one.|Separate physical counter units, register encoding and logical time.|The arithmetic fixture is not hardware programming evidence. Exact timer clock/prescaler/low-power behavior needs the selected device contract.
11.b|BCFG|Implement a tickless state machine with read-time,check-ready,program,recheck,sleep,wake and elapsed compensation. Inject a wake at every boundary, including immediately before sleep; then author the target timer adapter separately from the existing periodic SysTick path.|A next-deadline calculation or WFI in periodic idle is not complete tickless operation.|No injected wake strands runnable work; elapsed time is credited once and never expires early.|Model a timer stopping in sleep and derive an alternate always-on time source.|Treat sleep entry as a protocol with an explicit race closure.|The existing target remains periodic. Its successful guest receipt cannot close the new tickless hardware gate.
11.c|AFHI|Reconcile logical time with observed counter deltas across early wake,full expiry and long split waits. Keep energy measurements,board wake latency and emulator control-flow evidence separate.|Logical correctness does not imply lower energy or physical wake timing.|Every interval is counted once; unavailable physical measurements remain not-run, with exact required traces listed.|Change clock source and repeat conversion/calibration assumptions.|Use an independent timeline oracle for compensation.|A robust dossier includes clock drift and instrumentation assumptions. No hardware flashing is performed by curriculum authoring.
12.a|ABJ|Compose acquisition,CPU processing and deferred output using explicit capacities,task identities,release periods and bounded ISR handoff. State overload/drop/backpressure and task-failure policies before implementation.|Passing components individually does not guarantee compatible ownership or bounded integrated demand.|The composition has one owner per sample and explicit handling of every full/failure transition.|Replace copied samples with borrowed DMA buffers and derive completion/quiescence requirements.|Draw resource ownership across all component boundaries.|The cumulative task reuses original implementations; new credit concerns their interface interactions and failure behavior.
12.b|BFGH|Run a bounded overload burst and one task failure while safe peers continue; retain source/image/map identities,callback/queue/task traces and per-component RAM/CPU ledger. Reconcile fixture RAM14848/free1536 and cycles240 as an arithmetic model only.|A nominal pipeline checksum does not establish overload recovery or physical cycle cost.|Every sample is accounted as processed/rejected by policy; recovery does not exhaust reserved capacity; model totals are not reported as measured target costs.|Make diagnostic output backpressure coincide with recovery and preserve bounded behavior.|Reserve recovery resources before injecting failure.|Logging can consume the very resources needed for containment. The experiment must state its own observation cost and limits.
12.c|AFIJ|Prepare delayed independent integration with a changed task-lifetime or timer assumption. Require dependency audit,actual selected target trace,negative control and updated resource argument; retain physical timing as pending until measured.|A finite passing suite or assessor implementation does not certify absolute mastery.|The rubric distinguishes copied assembly/results from independent explanation and changed-condition implementation; evidence categories stay separate.|Port to another Cortex-M configuration and identify every ISA,ABI,FP and board premise that changes.|Reuse contracts only after checking each premise.|The advanced route requires independent and physical evidence beyond author validation; preserving those open obligations prevents false completion claims.
